---
// The home page in the glass build: Raymond's own pitch deck, twelve slides in the order a good
// seed deck runs (title, problem, insight, approach, demo, product, model, market, process,
// traction, team, ask). Each slide's notes say why it's built that way, so the deck teaches the
// method while it sells it. Prices come from src/content/services, the examples from src/content/work.
import { getServices, getWork, pad } from '../../lib/content';
import { site } from '../../site.config';
import { hears } from '../../lib/hears';
import Deck from './Deck.astro';
import Slide from './Slide.astro';
import RodCompare from './RodCompare.astro';
import FitPlan from './FitPlan.astro';

const services = await getServices();
const notes = await getWork('analysis');
const market = notes.find((n) => n.id === 'market-slide' && n.data.example) ?? notes.find((n) => n.data.example)!;
const money = (n: number) => `$${n.toLocaleString('en-US')}`;
const lo = Math.min(...services.map((s) => s.data.priceMin));
const hi = Math.max(...services.map((s) => s.data.priceMax));

const heard = notes.filter((n) => n.data.example);

const steps = [
  { when: 'Day 0', title: 'Enquiry', body: 'Tell me about the company, the round and where the story feels weak. Share the deck if you have one.' },
  { when: 'Within two business days', title: 'First read', body: 'I read everything myself and reply with a straight answer: whether I can help, which service fits, or whether you need me at all yet.' },
  { when: 'The work', title: 'The narrative', body: 'We agree the thesis in writing, then build or rebuild the storyline and the slides around it.' },
  { when: 'The raise', title: 'The room', body: 'You walk in able to explain the company and defend it. With Q&A preparation, you’ve already rehearsed the hard questions.' },
];

const N = 12;
---

<Deck name="main" label="The case for working with Raymond Ekpo">
  <!-- 01 -->
  <Slide id="title" title="Investors fund what they can explain" section="Investor narrative" n={1} of={N}>
    <div class="s1">
      <p class="d-kicker" data-b>The case for working with me, in twelve slides</p>
      <h1 class="d-display s1-title" data-b>Investors fund what they can <span class="d-acc">explain</span> to someone else.</h1>
      <div class="s1-foot" data-b>
        <p class="d-lead">
          I build the investment narrative behind pre-seed and seed rounds: the thesis, the storyline, and only then the
          deck. So this site is a deck.
        </p>
        <div class="d-btns">
          <a href="/work-with-me" class="d-btn" data-cta="Discuss your raise" data-cta-location="hero">Discuss your raise</a>
          <a href="/work-with-me?type=audit" class="d-link" data-cta="Request a free deck audit" data-cta-location="hero">Free deck audit</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="s1-hint d-fine" data-b>
        <span class="s1-hint-keys"><span class="d-key" aria-hidden="true">→</span> next slide &nbsp; <span class="d-key" aria-hidden="true">N</span> speaker notes</span>
        <span class="s1-hint-touch">Swipe for the next slide. Notes are in the bar below.</span>
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A title slide gets about ten seconds. It should say what the company does in words an investor could repeat, not a tagline.</p>
      <p>Mine is the sentence the rest of this deck tries to prove. Every slide after it has notes like these: why it’s built the way it is.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- 02 -->
  <Slide id="problem" title="The problem" section="02 · The problem" tone="ink" n={2} of={N}>
    <div class="s2">
      <h2 class="d-h1 s2-title" data-b>Most decks describe the company. <span class="d-dim">Few make the case for investing in it.</span></h2>
      <ol class="s2-points">
        <li data-b>
          <span class="d-num">01</span>
          <h3 class="d-h3">You know the product better than anyone.</h3>
          <p class="d-text">So the deck explains how it works. Investors needed to hear why it’s an investment, which is a different question.</p>
        </li>
        <li data-b>
          <span class="d-num">02</span>
          <h3 class="d-h3">Investors don’t decide alone.</h3>
          <p class="d-text">Before they commit, they explain your company to someone else: their partners, their co-investors, or their own judgement.</p>
        </li>
        <li data-b>
          <span class="d-num">03</span>
          <h3 class="d-h3">The retelling is where it breaks.</h3>
          <p class="d-text">A narrative has to survive being retold without you in the room. Most are built to be presented, not repeated.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A problem slide works when the reader already half-believes it. Name the pain in their words, then make it specific enough to be uncomfortable.</p>
      <p>Three points is the limit. A fourth is usually the first one again.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- 03 -->
  <Slide id="heard" title="What investors hear" section="03 · What investors hear" n={3} of={N}>
    <div class="s3">
      <div class="s3-head" data-b>
        <h2 class="d-h2">Five slides decide most first meetings. <span class="d-dim">Here’s how they’re heard.</span></h2>
      </div>
      <div class="s3-grid" data-b>
        <fieldset class="d-seg s3-tabs" data-seg="checked">
          <legend class="sr-only">Choose a slide</legend>
          <span class="d-seg-thumb" data-seg-thumb aria-hidden="true"></span>
          {
            heard.map((n, i) => (
              <label class="d-seg-item" data-seg-item>
                <input type="radio" name="heard" value={n.id} checked={i === 0} />
                <span>{n.data.example!.slide}</span>
              </label>
            ))
          }
        </fieldset>
        <div class="s3-views">
          {
            heard.map((n) => (
              <div class="s3-view" data-view={n.id}>
                <p class="d-kicker">You write</p>
                <p class="s3-write">“{n.data.example!.before.headline}”</p>
                <p class="d-kicker s3-hear-k">They hear</p>
                <p class="s3-hear">{hears[n.id] ?? n.data.dek}</p>
                <a href={`/work/${n.id}`} class="d-link">The analysis, and the rewrite</a>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>
      <style is:inline set:html={heard.map((n) => `.s3:has(input[value="${n.id}"]:checked) [data-view="${n.id}"]{display:block}`).join('')} />
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Investors read decks fast and match patterns. The same five slides carry most of the judgement, and each one is quietly answering a different question from the one the founder thinks it’s answering.</p>
      <p>The fix is rarely more information. It’s saying the thing they’re actually checking for.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- 04 -->
  <Slide id="approach" title="The approach" section="04 · The approach" tone="klein" n={4} of={N}>
    <div class="s4">
      <h2 class="d-h1 s4-title" data-b>Write the argument first. <span class="d-dim">Design it last.</span></h2>
      <ol class="s4-steps">
        <li data-b>
          <span class="d-num">1</span>
          <h3 class="d-h3">Thesis</h3>
          <p class="d-text">The investment case, written as a paragraph you could say out loud.</p>
        </li>
        <li data-b>
          <span class="d-num">2</span>
          <h3 class="d-h3">Storyline</h3>
          <p class="d-text">What goes first, what gets cut and what’s missing, so each claim sets up the next.</p>
        </li>
        <li data-b>
          <span class="d-num">3</span>
          <h3 class="d-h3">Slides</h3>
          <p class="d-text">One claim per slide, with the evidence under it. Design matters, but it’s the last step.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>If the investment case can’t be written as a clear paragraph, layout won’t fix it. So the writing comes before the design, and every slide has to earn its place in the argument.</p>
      <p>A solution slide should show how it works in as few moving parts as possible. Three is plenty.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- 05 -->
  <Slide id="before-after" title="Before and after" section="05 · Before & after" n={5} of={N}>
    <div class="s5">
      <div class="s5-copy">
        <h2 class="d-h2" data-b>One slide, rewritten. <span class="d-dim">Drag the glass.</span></h2>
        <p class="d-text" data-b>
          The original states the size of the world. The rewrite states the size of the company you can build from here,
          and shows the working.
        </p>
        <p class="d-fine" data-b>Illustrative. The company, numbers and events are invented to show the reasoning.</p>
        <p data-b><a href={`/work/${market.id}`} class="d-link">Read the analysis</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="s5-demo" data-b>
        <RodCompare example={market.data.example!} />
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Show the product working instead of describing it. Here, that’s one slide before and after.</p>
      <p>The rewrite claims less than the original and is far easier to believe. That’s the trade most decks need to make: a smaller number you can defend beats a bigger one nobody does.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- 06 -->
  <Slide id="engagements" title="Four engagements" section="06 · The product" n={6} of={N}>
    <div class="s6">
      <h2 class="d-h2" data-b>Four engagements. <span class="d-dim">Each with a clear end.</span></h2>
      <ol class="s6-cols">
        {
          services.map((s, i) => (
            <li data-b>
              <span class="d-num">{pad(i + 1)}</span>
              <h3 class="d-h3">{s.data.title}</h3>
              <p class="d-text s6-for">{s.data.for}</p>
              <p class="s6-price">{s.data.price}</p>
              <p class="d-fine">{s.data.priceNote}</p>
              <p class="d-text s6-get">{s.data.youGet}</p>
              <a href={`/services#${s.id}`} class="d-link">
                What’s included
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A product slide should show what someone buys, not every feature. Four options with fixed scopes and visible prices let a founder find their own fit in seconds.</p>
      <p>The full scope of each is in Appendix A.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- 07 -->
  <Slide id="model" title="Fixed fees" section="07 · Business model" tone="ink" n={7} of={N}>
    <div class="s7">
      <h2 class="d-h2 s7-title" data-b>Fixed fees, published. <span class="d-dim">Nothing scales with your raise.</span></h2>
      <dl class="s7-stats">
        <div data-b>
          <dt class="s7-big">0%</dt>
          <dd class="d-text">of your round. No success fees, and I don’t sell introductions to investors.</dd>
        </div>
        <div data-b>
          <dt class="s7-big">{money(lo)}–{money(hi)}</dt>
          <dd class="d-text">The range of every fee, all of them listed. You get a fixed quote before anything starts.</dd>
        </div>
        <div data-b>
          <dt class="s7-big">1</dt>
          <dd class="d-text">person does the work: me. Whoever reads your enquiry writes your narrative. Nothing is handed to someone junior.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Investors look for a model they can explain in a sentence. Founders buying a service want the same thing.</p>
      <p>Big numbers on a model slide should be facts the reader can check, so these are: the fees are all on this site, and none of them moves with the size of your raise.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- 08 -->
  <Slide id="fit" title="Who it’s for" section="08 · Who it’s for" n={8} of={N}>
    <FitPlan />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A good market slide says exactly who buys. This is mine, as five questions.</p>
      <p>If you’re raising pre-seed or seed, it drafts the engagements that fit, in order, with what they cost. If you’re raising Series A or later, it says plainly that I’m probably not the right person.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- 09 -->
  <Slide id="process" title="How it works" section="09 · How it works" n={9} of={N}>
    <div class="s9">
      <h2 class="d-h2" data-b>From first message <span class="d-dim">to the room.</span></h2>
      <ol class="s9-line">
        {
          steps.map((s, i) => (
            <li data-b>
              <span class="s9-dot" aria-hidden="true" />
              <p class="d-kicker">{s.when}</p>
              <h3 class="d-h3">
                <span class="d-num">{i + 1}</span> {s.title}
              </h3>
              <p class="d-text">{s.body}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Investors want to see the path from today to the next milestone. Founders want the same from anyone they hire.</p>
      <p>Each step says who does what, and when. The first one has a deadline I can be held to.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- 10 -->
  <Slide id="traction" title="Traction" section="10 · Traction" tone="klein" n={10} of={N}>
    <div class="s10">
      <h2 class="d-h1 s10-title" data-b>No client logos. <span class="d-dim">On purpose.</span></h2>
      <div class="s10-grid">
        <p class="d-lead" data-b>
          Fundraising is confidential, and most founders would rather their deck wasn’t public while they’re raising.
          Client work appears here only with the founder’s permission, and only with outcomes that can be checked.
        </p>
        <div class="s10-side" data-b>
          <p class="d-kicker">Until then, the thinking is public</p>
          <ol class="s10-notes">
            {
              notes.map((n) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={`/work/${n.id}`}>
                    <span>{n.data.topic}</span>
                    {n.data.title}
                  </a>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ol>
          <a href="/work-with-me" class="d-btn" data-cta="Ask for examples" data-cta-location="deck traction">Ask for examples</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The traction slide is the one most decks inflate. Don’t. An unverifiable claim costs more trust than a modest one, and investors check.</p>
      <p>When there’s nothing you can show yet, say why, and show the next best evidence. Here that’s five analyses of the slides that decide most first meetings.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- 11 -->
  <Slide id="team" title="Team" section="11 · Team" n={11} of={N}>
    <div class="s11">
      <div class="s11-id" data-b>
        {
          site.portrait ? (
            <img src={site.portrait} alt={site.name} class="s11-photo" width="640" height="800" />
          ) : (
            <span class="s11-mono" aria-hidden="true">
              RE
            </span>
          )
        }
        <p class="d-h3 s11-name">{site.name}</p>
        <p class="d-fine">{site.role}</p>
        <dl class="s11-facts">
          <div>
            <dt>Works with</dt>
            <dd>Pre-seed and seed founders</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Raising from</dt>
            <dd>US and UK investors</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Also</dt>
            <dd>
              Founder, <a href={site.firm.url} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{site.firm.name}</a>
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div class="s11-copy">
        <h2 class="d-h2" data-b>I work on the part of a raise <span class="d-dim">that happens before the slides.</span></h2>
        <p class="d-text" data-b>
          I spent several years studying how markets work, mostly by watching capable companies stall for reasons nobody
          inside them could name. That work became {site.firm.name}, the growth strategy firm I run.
        </p>
        <p class="d-text" data-b>
          The same problem turns up at the very start of a company. A founder knows the product, the customers and the
          market better than anyone, and still struggles to say why it’s an investment.
        </p>
        <p data-b><a href="/about" class="d-link">More about how I work</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A team slide answers one question: why are these the people to do this?</p>
      <p>Relevant history, said plainly, does more than a wall of logos. The history here is the job itself: finding what’s actually in the way, and writing it down in a form people can argue with.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- 12 -->
  <Slide id="ask" title="The ask" section="12 · The ask" tone="ink" n={12} of={N}>
    <div class="s12">
      <h2 class="d-h1 s12-title" data-b>Raising in the next six months? <span class="d-acc">Let’s look at your narrative.</span></h2>
      <div class="s12-asks">
        <a href="/work-with-me" class="s12-ask" data-b data-cta="Discuss your raise" data-cta-location="deck ask">
          <span class="d-kicker">The ask</span>
          <span class="d-h3">Discuss your raise</span>
          <span class="d-text">Tell me about the company, the round and where the story feels weak.</span>
          <span class="s12-go" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/work-with-me?type=audit" class="s12-ask" data-b data-cta="Request a free deck audit" data-cta-location="deck ask">
          <span class="d-kicker">Or start free</span>
          <span class="d-h3">Request a mini-audit</span>
          <span class="d-text">Three changes I’d make to your current deck, in writing.</span>
          <span class="s12-go" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="d-fine s12-fine" data-b>
        I read every enquiry myself and reply within {site.replyTime}. Nobody can promise you a raise, and I don’t. ·{' '}
        <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`} data-contact="email">{site.email}</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>End with a specific ask: how much, for what, and what it proves.</p>
      <p>Mine is small on purpose. One conversation or a free audit, and within two business days you’ll know whether I can help.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>
</Deck>

<style>
  /* 01 title */
  .s1 {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(18px, 3.4vh, 40px);
  }

  .s1-title {
    max-width: 10.5em;
  }

  .s1-foot {
    display: grid;
    gap: 24px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1000px) {
    .s1-foot {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
      align-items: end;
      gap: 48px;
    }
  }

  .s1-hint-touch {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (hover: none) {
    .s1-hint-keys {
      display: none;
    }
    .s1-hint-touch {
      display: inline;
    }
  }

  /* 02 problem */
  .s2 {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(28px, 7vh, 80px);
  }

  .s2-title {
    max-width: 12em;
  }

  .s2-points,
  .s4-steps,
  .s6-cols,
  .s9-line,
  .s10-notes {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .s2-points {
    display: grid;
    gap: 22px 40px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .s2-points {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  .s2-points li {
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
    align-content: start;
    padding-top: 16px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-c);
  }

  /* 03 heard */
  .s3 {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(22px, 5vh, 56px);
  }

  .s3-head .d-h2 {
    max-width: 15em;
  }

  .s3-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 26px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .s3-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(200px, 3fr) minmax(0, 9fr);
      gap: clamp(32px, 5vw, 80px);
      align-items: start;
    }
    .s3-grid .s3-tabs {
      grid-auto-flow: row;
      grid-auto-rows: 1fr;
    }
    .s3-grid .s3-tabs :global(.d-seg-item) {
      justify-content: flex-start;
      min-height: 48px;
      padding: 0 16px;
      font-size: 1rem;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .s3-grid .s3-tabs :global(.d-seg-item) {
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      padding: 0 4px;
    }
  }

  .s3-view {
    display: none;
    animation: d-build 0.55s var(--ease) both;
  }

  .s3-write {
    margin: 10px 0 0;
    font: 500 var(--t-h3) / 1.15 var(--text);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--dim);
    text-decoration: line-through;
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
    text-decoration-color: rgb(31 47 214 / 0.55);
  }

  .s3-hear-k {
    margin-top: clamp(18px, 3.4vh, 34px);
  }

  .s3-hear {
    margin: 10px 0 clamp(18px, 3vh, 28px);
    max-width: 24em;
    font: 800 clamp(1.5rem, min(2.9vw, 4.8vh), 3.1rem) / 1.04 var(--disp);
    letter-spacing: -0.035em;
    color: var(--acc);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* 04 approach */
  .s4 {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(30px, 8vh, 90px);
  }

  .s4-steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: 22px 40px;
    counter-reset: step;
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .s4-steps {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  .s4-steps li {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    align-content: start;
    padding-top: 18px;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--fg);
  }

  .s4-steps .d-num {
    font-size: var(--t-h2);
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .s4-steps li:not(:last-child)::after {
      content: '→';
      position: absolute;
      right: -30px;
      top: -0.55em;
      font: 500 1.4rem/1 var(--text);
      color: var(--fg);
    }
  }

  /* 05 before and after */
  .s5 {
    display: grid;
    gap: 28px;
    align-items: center;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1000px) {
    .s5 {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 4fr) minmax(0, 8fr);
      gap: clamp(36px, 5vw, 80px);
    }
  }

  .s5-copy {
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
    align-content: center;
  }

  /* 06 engagements */
  .s6 {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(26px, 6vh, 64px);
  }

  .s6-cols {
    display: grid;
    gap: 28px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .s6-cols {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 1100px) {
    .s6-cols {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 0;
    }
    .s6-cols li {
      padding: 0 clamp(16px, 1.6vw, 28px);
      border-left: 1px solid var(--rule-c);
    }
    .s6-cols li:first-child {
      padding-left: 0;
      border-left: 0;
    }
  }

  .s6-cols li {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .s6-for {
